-- Runtime grants for the restricted application role.
--
-- Apply as the schema owner, after migrations.
-- Replace __APP_ROLE__ using the deployment's secret-safe templating mechanism.
-- Do not grant CREATE or schema ownership to the runtime role.
--
-- Covers the **final retained Phase 2 schema only**. An earlier revision of this
-- template also granted `character_state_values`, `character_balances` and
-- `character_transactions`; ADR 0008 was rejected on 2026-08-02 and those tables
-- no longer exist. A grant naming a dropped table is harmless but a missing
-- REVOKE on a retained append-only table is not, which is why the two lists
-- below are checked against the live schema by `tests/test_runtime_grants.py`
-- rather than maintained by eye.
--
-- ## PUBLIC (finding O-1)
--
-- An earlier revision claimed its `REVOKE ... FROM __APP_ROLE__` statements
-- "handled rights held by PUBLIC". They did not, and could not: revoking from a
-- role does not touch a privilege the role holds *through* PUBLIC. Every member
-- of PUBLIC is every role in the cluster, the runtime role included, so a single
-- `GRANT ... ON audit_events TO PUBLIC` — from a migration, a recovery session
-- or a well-meaning operator — would have given the runtime role UPDATE and
-- DELETE on append-only history with nothing here to take it away.
--
-- The fix is the explicit normalisation below: every retained table has all
-- privileges revoked from PUBLIC before the intended grants are issued. This is
-- a no-op on a clean cluster, which is the point — it makes the ACL state a
-- property of applying this template rather than of nothing ever having gone
-- wrong. `tests/test_runtime_grants_live.py` seeds hostile PUBLIC grants,
-- applies this file, and then asserts the *effective* privileges through
-- `has_table_privilege`, so the evidence is what PostgreSQL concludes rather
-- than what this text says.

GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO __APP_ROLE__;

-- PUBLIC gets no ability to create objects in the schema. (PostgreSQL 15 and
-- later no longer grant this by default; it is stated here so the template is
-- also correct against an older cluster or one that has drifted.)
REVOKE CREATE ON SCHEMA public FROM PUBLIC;

-- The list below covers the Phase 1-2 schema and the Phase 3 identity, session
-- and credential tables added by migration 0006. It is checked against the live
-- schema by `tests/test_runtime_grants.py`, which parses these statements, so
-- keep the table lists free of inline comments.
--
-- Normalise PUBLIC on every retained table, before granting anything. Ordering
-- matters: a REVOKE issued after the GRANTs below would still be correct, but
-- reading it in this order is what makes "the runtime role holds exactly what
-- the next two statements give it" true on the page as well as in the cluster.
REVOKE ALL PRIVILEGES ON
    discord_users,
    discord_guild_memberships,
    discord_membership_roles,
    characters,
    character_access,
    external_actor_mappings,
    sheet_row_mappings,
    idempotency_keys,
    submission_admissions,
    audit_events,
    foundry_snapshots,
    snapshot_imports,
    platform_initialization,
    platform_accounts,
    external_identities,
    oauth_token_grants,
    sessions,
    oauth_transactions,
    webauthn_credentials,
    webauthn_challenges,
    recovery_grants,
    auth_rate_limits,
    role_capability_mappings,
    role_capability_mapping_events,
    identity_migration_runs,
    identity_link_proposals,
    identity_link_proposal_candidates,
    snapshot_folder_selections,
    reconciliation_jobs,
    reconciliation_job_results
FROM PUBLIC;

-- Operational tables: the application creates, reads and maintains these.
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON
    discord_users,
    discord_guild_memberships,
    discord_membership_roles,
    characters,
    character_access,
    external_actor_mappings,
    sheet_row_mappings,
    idempotency_keys
TO __APP_ROLE__;

-- `role_capability_mapping_events` joins the append-only set here: it records
-- every attempted role-capability change including refusals (schema §8.1), and
-- the service whose behaviour it records must not be able to edit it — the same
-- rule, for the same reason, as `audit_events`.
--
-- Append-only tables. The application exposes no update or delete use case for
-- any of these, the runtime role is not granted one, and a database trigger
-- refuses UPDATE and DELETE even for the schema owner (migration 0002).
--
-- TRUNCATE is revoked here rather than trigger-blocked, so the owner can still
-- reset a disposable test database while the runtime role cannot empty history.
-- The admission fence (migration 0005). SELECT only, and the omission is the
-- control rather than an oversight: the application must be able to *read*
-- whether its credential's generation is still open, and must not be able to
-- open, close or reopen one. Opening and closing are operator actions taken by
-- `tools.submission_admission` as the schema owner.
--
-- No INSERT either. A runtime role that could insert an admission could admit
-- itself, which would make the fence advisory. `tests/test_runtime_grants_live.py`
-- proves all four denials against PostgreSQL rather than reading this comment.
GRANT SELECT ON submission_admissions TO __APP_ROLE__;
REVOKE INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON submission_admissions FROM __APP_ROLE__;

GRANT SELECT, INSERT ON
    audit_events,
    foundry_snapshots,
    snapshot_imports,
    platform_initialization,
    role_capability_mapping_events
TO __APP_ROLE__;

-- REVOKE after GRANT is deliberate and not redundant: it is what makes the
-- append-only set explicit at the point a reader is looking for it, and it
-- would take back an UPDATE or DELETE mistakenly added to the GRANT above.
REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON audit_events FROM __APP_ROLE__;
REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON foundry_snapshots FROM __APP_ROLE__;
REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON snapshot_imports FROM __APP_ROLE__;
-- The supervised first-import bootstrap writes this row once and nothing may
-- change it again; the singleton primary key is what makes that irreversible.
REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON platform_initialization FROM __APP_ROLE__;
REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON role_capability_mapping_events FROM __APP_ROLE__;

-- ## Phase 3 identity, session and credential tables (schema §11.2)
--
-- Three bands, and the boundaries between them are the design rather than an
-- accident of what each service happens to call.
--
-- **No DELETE.** An account is `closed` and an identity is `retired`, never
-- deleted, because audit attribution resolves through them after a provider is
-- retired (ADR 0010 D4). A session, a mapping and a credential are revoked,
-- expired or disabled in place, so the history of who could do what stays
-- readable. A recovery grant is consumed, not removed: the audit event is the
-- permanent record, but the grant row is what an operator compares a suspected
-- compromise against while it is still retained.
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE ON
    platform_accounts,
    external_identities,
    sessions,
    oauth_transactions,
    webauthn_credentials,
    recovery_grants,
    role_capability_mappings
TO __APP_ROLE__;
REVOKE DELETE, TRUNCATE ON
    platform_accounts,
    external_identities,
    sessions,
    oauth_transactions,
    webauthn_credentials,
    recovery_grants,
    role_capability_mappings
FROM __APP_ROLE__;

-- **DELETE, deliberately.** These three hold nothing that is evidence of
-- anything after its window closes, and two of them must be deletable as a
-- control rather than as a convenience:
--
-- - `oauth_token_grants` — N-11 requires the stored provider tokens to be
--   *deleted* on logout, on revocation and no later than the session's absolute
--   expiry. Token deletion is a privacy control that outranks the forensic value
--   of keeping the row, and it is the reason this table is not append-only.
-- - `auth_rate_limits` — fixed windows older than N-31 are swept opportunistically
--   so the table cannot grow without bound; a counter has no history to lose.
-- - `webauthn_challenges` — a spent or expired challenge is a public nonce whose
--   whole life is one login attempt. The audit record names the credential, not
--   the challenge.
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON
    oauth_token_grants,
    auth_rate_limits,
    webauthn_challenges
TO __APP_ROLE__;
REVOKE TRUNCATE ON
    oauth_token_grants,
    auth_rate_limits,
    webauthn_challenges
FROM __APP_ROLE__;

-- ## Phase 3 identity-evidence proposals (P3.2, migration 0010)
--
-- **DELETE, deliberately**, and it is schema §11.2's classification for
-- `identity_link_proposals` rather than a new judgement: the row is *evidence*
-- with a 90-day retention, not a record of anything that happened. The
-- authorization a confirmation creates is a `character_access` row and its audit
-- event, and both are outside this band — the access row cannot be deleted at
-- all, and the audit event cannot be touched. So a purge that has run its course
-- removes the working papers and leaves the decision.
--
-- The two tables schema §11.2 does not name inherit the same band for the same
-- reason: a run is what proposals hang from and a candidate is what an ambiguous
-- proposal is made of, so a retention sweep that could remove one and not the
-- others would leave the evidence half-readable. The `ON DELETE CASCADE` from
-- run to proposal to candidate is what makes one delete statement enough.
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON
    identity_migration_runs,
    identity_link_proposals,
    identity_link_proposal_candidates
TO __APP_ROLE__;
REVOKE TRUNCATE ON
    identity_migration_runs,
    identity_link_proposals,
    identity_link_proposal_candidates
FROM __APP_ROLE__;

-- ## Phase 3 durable reconciliation jobs (P3.3, migration 0011)
--
-- Schema §11.2 classifies the two job tables directly:
-- `reconciliation_jobs` is `SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE` and
-- `reconciliation_job_results` is `SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE`.
--
-- **No DELETE on `reconciliation_jobs`, deliberately.** N-24 retains a terminal
-- job for 30 days and then removes it, and that sweep is an operator action run
-- as the schema owner (`python -m tools.job_retention`), not something the web
-- process or the worker may do to its own queue. A runtime role that could
-- delete a job could delete the record of a refused apply. The `ON DELETE
-- CASCADE` from job to result is what makes the operator's one statement remove
-- the result with it; the result table's own DELETE is the worker replacing a
-- superseded result, which is why it has one and the job does not.
--
-- **`snapshot_folder_selections` is `SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE`**, matching the band
-- schema §11.2 gives every table whose row is changed in place rather than
-- appended to: R-41 replaces the selection, and the append-only history of who
-- selected what is the `snapshot.folder_selected` audit event, which is in the
-- band above and cannot be touched at all.
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE ON
    snapshot_folder_selections,
    reconciliation_jobs
TO __APP_ROLE__;
REVOKE DELETE, TRUNCATE ON
    snapshot_folder_selections,
    reconciliation_jobs
FROM __APP_ROLE__;

GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON
    reconciliation_job_results
TO __APP_ROLE__;
REVOKE TRUNCATE ON
    reconciliation_job_results
FROM __APP_ROLE__;

-- Sequences: none of the retained tables uses one. Identifiers are application
-- generated UUIDs (ADR 0003, OD-35), so no sequence privilege is granted.

-- ## The schema-version table (S-14), added 2026-08-23 by P3.5
--
-- `alembic_version` is **not** an application table and is not in the ORM
-- metadata, which is why every list above is checked against that metadata and
-- this statement stands apart from them.
--
-- It is here because the portal refuses to start without reading it. S-14 is
-- "a process that cannot establish which schema it is serving must not serve
-- one", and it establishes that by selecting `version_num` over the *runtime*
-- connection. Without this grant the restricted role can never start the
-- portal at all — which is exactly what happened the first time the service
-- ran under the restricted role, and which no test caught because no test had
-- ever run the deployed configuration (I-06, TC-OPS-04).
--
-- SELECT only, and deliberately: migrations run as the schema owner, and a
-- runtime role that could write this table could tell the next startup it was
-- serving a schema it was not.
REVOKE ALL PRIVILEGES ON alembic_version FROM PUBLIC;
REVOKE ALL PRIVILEGES ON alembic_version FROM __APP_ROLE__;
GRANT SELECT ON alembic_version TO __APP_ROLE__;
